4.9 Key functions
The main functions covered in this chapter:








Function
Package
What it does




read_csv()
{readr}
Loads a .csv file as a data frame (tibble).


read.csv()
{utils}
Base R’s version of read_csv().


setwd()
{base}
Sets the working directory using an absolute path. Avoid it in .qmd files.


list.files()
{base}
Lists the files in a directory, optionally only those matching a pattern.


list.dirs()
{base}
Lists the directories inside a directory.


dir.exists()
{base}
Checks whether a directory exists.


file.exists()
{base}
Checks whether a file exists.


dir.create()
{base}
Creates a new directory.


View()
{utils}
Opens a data frame in RStudio’s data viewer.


print()
{base}
Prints an object below the chunk.


kable()
{knitr}
Formats a data frame as a table.


kable_styling()
{kableExtra}
Styles a table made with kable().


write_csv()
{readr}
Saves a data frame as a .csv file.


write.csv(row.names = FALSE)
{utils}
Base R’s version of write_csv(). Set row.names = FALSE to avoid adding an extra column.


read_excel()
{readxl}
Loads a sheet from an Excel file, with sheet and skip arguments.


head()
{utils}
Shows the first few rows of a data frame.


bind_rows()
{dplyr}
Stacks data frames on top of each other, matching columns by name.


write_rds()
{readr}
Saves an R object as a .rds file.


read_rds()
{readr}
Loads an R object from a .rds file.


map_dfr()
{purrr}
Applies a function (e.g., read_csv()) to each element of a list and binds the results into one data frame.

6.6 Key functions
The main functions covered in this chapter:








Function
Package
What it does




select()
{dplyr}
Keeps or drops columns; can also rename and reorder them.


starts_with()
{tidyselect}
Selects columns whose names start with a string.


ends_with()
{tidyselect}
Selects columns whose names end with a string.


contains()
{tidyselect}
Selects columns whose names contain a string anywhere.


all_of()
{tidyselect}
Selects all columns named in a character vector; errors if any are missing.


any_of()
{tidyselect}
Selects the columns named in a character vector that are present, silently skipping missing ones.


where()
{tidyselect}
Selects columns that meet a criterion, e.g., where(is.numeric).


everything()
{tidyselect}
Selects all columns.


relocate()
{dplyr}
Moves columns to a new position, using .before or .after.


last_col()
{tidyselect}
Refers to the last column, e.g., in relocate(.after = last_col()).


filter()
{dplyr}
Keeps rows that meet a logical test.


%in%
{base}
Tests whether each value is equal to one of a set of values.


mutate()
{dplyr}
Creates new columns or changes existing ones.


as.logical()
{base}
Converts values to logical (TRUE/FALSE).


str_to_lower()
{stringr}
Converts text to lower case.


str_remove_all()
{stringr}
Removes all matches of a pattern (regex) from text.


if_else()
{dplyr}
Returns one value if a condition is TRUE and another if it is FALSE.


case_when()
{dplyr}
Applies several if-else rules in order, returning the value for the first one that is met.

7.9 Key functions
The main functions covered in this chapter:








Function
Package
What it does




arrange()
{dplyr}
Changes the order of the rows, by one or more columns.


desc()
{dplyr}
Used inside arrange() to sort in descending order.


slice_head(), slice_tail()
{dplyr}
Keeps the first or last n rows.


slice_min(), slice_max()
{dplyr}
Keeps the rows with the lowest or highest values of a column (ties are kept by default).


slice_sample()
{dplyr}
Keeps a random sample of rows.


set.seed()
{base}
Makes random processes, such as slice_sample(), reproducible.


is.na()
{base}
Tests whether each value is NA.


drop_na()
{tidyr}
Drops rows with NA in the named columns (or in any column, if none are named).


na_if()
{dplyr}
Converts a specific value, e.g., a missing data code like -99, to NA.


replace_na()
{tidyr}
Replaces NA with a specific value.


across()
{dplyr}
Applies the same function to many columns inside mutate().


if_any(), if_all()
{dplyr}
Tests whether a condition is TRUE in any or all of a set of columns.


pick()
{dplyr}
Selects a set of columns inside mutate(), e.g., to pass to rowMeans().


rowSums(), rowMeans()
{base}
Calculates the sum or mean of each row, across columns.


add_n_miss(), add_prop_miss()
{naniar}
Adds columns with the number or proportion of missing values in each row.


miss_var_summary()
{naniar}
Summarizes the missingness in each column.


vis_miss()
{naniar}
Plots which cells of a data frame are missing.


snuffle_sum_scores()
{truffle}
Wrapper that cleans, reverse scores, and calculates sum scores for a scale.


lag(), lead()
{dplyr}
Returns the value from the previous or next row.


row_number()
{dplyr}
Returns the row number.


cumsum()
{base}
Returns the cumulative (running) sum.


fill()
{tidyr}
Fills NAs with the last non-missing value above (or below).

10.7 Key functions
The main functions covered in this chapter:








Function
Package
What it does




class()
{base}
Returns the type (class) of an object or column.


glimpse()
{dplyr}
Shows every column of a data frame with its type and first values.


as.numeric(), as.character(), as.logical(), as.integer()
{base}
Convert values to another type. Values that can’t be converted become NA, with a warning.


sprintf()
{base}
Formats numbers as text, e.g., sprintf("%.20f", x) shows the stored value of x to 20 decimal places.


near()
{dplyr}
Tests whether two numbers are equal within a small tolerance. Use it instead of == for decimal numbers.


between()
{dplyr}
Tests whether values are within a range, including the boundaries.


round()
{base}
Rounds numbers using banker’s rounding (.5 is rounded to the nearest even number).


round_up()
{roundwork}
Rounds .5 upwards, as most readers expect, without being affected by floating point representation.


round_p_value()
{truffle}
Formats p-values in APA style, as text.


is.na()
{base}
Tests whether each value is missing. Use it instead of == NA.


NA_real_, NA_character_, NA_integer_
{base}
Typed missing values, which make the intended type of a column explicit.

11.5 Key functions
The main functions covered in this chapter:








Function
Package
What it does




str_to_lower(), str_to_upper(), str_to_title(), str_to_sentence()
{stringr}
Change the case of letters.


str_trim(), str_squish()
{stringr}
Remove whitespace at the start and end; str_squish() also reduces repeated spaces inside the string to one.


str_length()
{stringr}
Counts the number of characters in each string.


str_c()
{stringr}
Combines strings, like base R’s paste0().


str_pad()
{stringr}
Pads strings to a fixed width, e.g., adds leading zeros to ids.


str_detect(), str_starts(), str_ends()
{stringr}
Test whether each string contains, starts with, or ends with a pattern.


str_remove(), str_remove_all()
{stringr}
Remove the first match, or every match, of a pattern.


str_replace(), str_replace_all()
{stringr}
Replace the first match, or every match, of a pattern.


str_extract()
{stringr}
Returns the part of each string that matches a pattern (or one of its groups).


str_sub()
{stringr}
Extracts characters by position.


str_view()
{stringr}
Shows which parts of each string match a pattern. Useful for testing regular expressions.


regex()
{stringr}
Modifies a pattern, e.g., with ignore_case = TRUE or comments = TRUE.


word()
{stringr}
Extracts words by position.


factor(), fct()
{base}, {forcats}
Create a factor. fct() throws an error for values that are not in the levels, rather than silently turning them into NA.


levels()
{base}
Returns the levels of a factor, in order.


fct_relevel(), fct_infreq(), fct_reorder(), fct_rev()
{forcats}
Change the order of the levels: move levels to the front, order by frequency, order by another variable, or reverse.


fct_recode(), fct_collapse()
{forcats}
Rename levels, or combine several levels into one.


fct_lump_n(), fct_lump_min()
{forcats}
Combine rare levels into an “Other” level.


fct_na_value_to_level()
{forcats}
Turns NA into an explicit level.


fct_drop()
{forcats}
Removes unused levels.


as.numeric(as.character())
{base}
Converts a factor that contains numbers to the numbers themselves, rather than the internal level codes.

